WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.480
Trevor “TmarTn” Martin

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:06.880
(Stream)

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.820
and Tom “ProSyndicate” Cassell

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.300
(Stream)

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:17.040
are two popular YouTubers, who, in late 2015, came up with an incredible scheme to make them rich.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:23.000
(Stream)

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.740
They were going to run a classic casino scam.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.580
(Stream)

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:32.120
Specifically, they were going to get people hooked on a CS:GO gambling site that they owned

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:34.440
but not tell anybody that they owned it.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.580
"We found this new site, called CS:GO Lotto."

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.820
It had all the makings of a great scam.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:45.540
"Dude you could just swing for the fences with one bet, and just come way up five grand."

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.320
The only problem was that CS:GO gambling was a massive

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:54.280
underground industry that chugged along in the dark alleys beneath the squeaky clean exterior of CS:GO

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:54.880
esports.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.860
And these two dinguses managed to explode and expose

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:01.660
one of gaming's biggest grey markets with just a few simple videos.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:26.400
(Stream)

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.100
It all started in December 2015, when TmarTn incorporated CSGO Lotto,

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.760
a CS:GO skin gambling site.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.640
Now, while the trend has died down a little, CS:GO skin gambling was all the rage just a few years ago.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.720
See, every skin is bought and sold on the steam marketplace which gives it a real world value.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:48.000
Now if you sold a skin on the steam marketplace right now you'd get steam credit but,

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:52.600
there are plenty of ways to turn those skins into cold hard cash.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.120
There were two primary ways to gamble with skins.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.720
You could either bet on the results of an esports match, or play a game, like roulette.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:10.800
(Stream)

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:16.040
But cashing out through these gambling sites and steam bots, only gave you the value you won in skins.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.460
The real game came in the skin grey market,

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:23.440
websites that let you trade your skins to someone else in exchange for money through say, PayPal.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.480
It was just like a real casino.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.440
Well... almost.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.980
Basically you turn your real human Earth money into skins.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:35.960
Either by buying them in the steam marketplace, or buying keys to open crates that contain them.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.760
You took your skins to the gambling site of your choice,

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.880
traded them to their bots for a new, fake currency that was roughly equivalent to US dollars,

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.720
which they were your casino chips.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:50.160
You then bet that money with that currency on either matches or roulette spins to win more money.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.540
Which you then turned back into skins.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:57.940
And finally, you sold those skins to someone through another site to get back real human Earth money.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.800
And now that you understand how skin gambling works, you might be asking to yourself,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.160
"How was all this legal?"

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.620
Well, it wasn't. At least, not technically.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:11.260
See, a lot of these sites skirted around gambling regulations by arguing that technically,

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:12.820
you weren’t gambling with money.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.580
You were gambling with a virtual item.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:19.100
And while it had real-world value, you could technically get it through a random crate or a drop.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:22.840
And then, you weren’t winning money, just skins. And yes,

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:27.980
skins do have real world value, but Valve didn’t let you get that money natively through Steam.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.220
We’ll be coming back to the legality of all this later on,

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:35.580
but for now remember that CS:GO, a game with a massive underage audience

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.880
had a powerful and prevalent underground gambling scene.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.740
So TmarTn and his associates, including another popular YouTuber, ProSyndicate,

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:47.240
Started CS:GO Lotto.com. A skin-gambling site to profit off of the craze.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:49.820
But they took it one step further.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.860
You don’t make money if no one is gambling at your casino,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.860
but advertising is expensive.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:57.000
And CS:GO Lotto had plenty of competition.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.300
Meanwhile ProSyndicate and TmarTn had massive YouTube channels.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:03.200
Each with millions of subscribers.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.800
If they could get the word about CS:GO Lotto to their huge audiences,

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.340
they were guaranteed to see at least some success.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:21.960
(Stream)

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:26.820
But, more importantly neither YouTuber mentioned the fact they were part-owners of CS:GO Lotto.com.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:29.000
They just kept making videos.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.640
Winning money and losing money on roulette spins and coin flips just like they were anybody else

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:35.620
and not part-owners of the website.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:41.960
(Stream)

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:43.220
But someone noticed.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.900
A YouTuber called HonorTheCall released a video on June 27, 2016

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:53.120
that first made the allegations that Tmartn and ProSyndicate were part-owners of CS:GO Lotto.com

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:58.660
"Hold onto your pants it's about to go down, this may be the biggest scandal in CoD community

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.600
which has nothing to do with Call of Duty."

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:06.180
"Let me be clear here, I'm not accusing anyone, I found some info and I want to show it to my viewers."

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:11.560
"So TmarTn and ProSyndicate visit a CS:GO gambling site called CS:GO Lotto.com.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.140
I did some digging into this and holy sh*t balls."

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:22.260
"This site was registered as a business in Orlando Florida, I thought it's just a coincidence

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:29.560
since TmarTn also lives in Orlando. But no it wasn't, Trevor Martin, aka TmarTn is the director of

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.440
this site, he f***ing owns the damn site."

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:37.720
He also accused the pair of faking their reactions to their big wins and losses in their YouTube videos.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:44.000
"These two on the website CS:GO Lotto.com and they allegedly do fake gambling and pretend to win

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.040
thousands of dollars and also sometimes pretend to lose it too."

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:52.340
Those claims were further backed up by popular YouTube channel H3H3 Productions,

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.420
who released a video going over HonorTheCall’s allegations

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:59.740
and pointed out that whether or not TmarTn and ProSyndicate were manipulating the results,

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:02.500
the fact that they could just wasn't a good look.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:08.580
"So here he fairy tale story, you know, 50 dollars up to thousands."

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:10.300
"In a thirteen minute video."

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.040
"Terrific stuff here guys."

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:25.220
(Stream)

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:31.740
"Do you think it's possible he faked his reaction and faked the actul results of the bet?"

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:34.280
"It's possible, it's very possible."

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:38.280
"I don't know if he did but it's possible, and that's why you don't gamble on your own site."

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:40.760
"It doesn't need to be sent, it's just, it's obscene."

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:44.480
So with all the allegations out there, how did TmarTn and ProSyndicate react?

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:50.020
Well Syndicate took the high ground and apologized and promised to be more transparent in the future but

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:55.140
he's been in trouble for doing things like this before, so it remains to be seen how genuine that was.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:57.080
But TmarTn doubled down.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.220
"What's going on guys, TmarT here and I hope you guys having a fantastic Monday

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.140
I just wanted to make a quick little vlog to talk to you guys and to kinda lay everything out

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:09.940
on the line. Give you guys the full true story straight from the source.

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:15.300
Because unfortunately, and I really, I don't understand kind of where it's coming from but

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:19.360
threre is gonna be a little bit of a sh*tstorm headed this way and some of the drama channels are gonna

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:22.380
pick up on it. I'm sure a lot of the hater channels are gonna pick up on it.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:26.200
But I wanted you guys to know exactly what's going on so you guys can make your own

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:30.200
well-informed decisions. So basically what's breaking news now,

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:34.160
is that myself and a few other people including Tom or ProSyndicate

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:40.800
own CS:GO Lotto. And this is something that has never been a secret. I don't understand why this is big breaking

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.500
news now because it's never been under wraps. It's never been a secret."

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.860
The problem is that it was a secret.

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:48.960
At least to his viewers.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.560
And that caught the attention of the United States Federal Trade Commission,

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:55.180
who didn't take kindly to the whole situation.

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:58.520
And launched and investigation into both TmarTn and ProSyndicate.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:02.960
A few days after that. Tmartn released an apology video that he quickly took down,

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:06.020
Probably because the FTC was already after him.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.740
"Cooper I have no idea how I'm gonna record this video dude."

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:21.880
"Now my connection to CS:GO Lotto has been a matter of public record since the company was

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:25.160
first organized in December of 2015."

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:30.740
"However I do feel like I owe you guys an apology. I am sorry to each and every one of you

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:33.700
who felt like that was not made clear enough to you."

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:37.200
The FTC taking interest in two YouTubers sparked headlines

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.080
that made some people noticed that Valve appeared to be enabling underage gambling.

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:44.880
Which led some people to file class-action lawsuits against Valve.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:46.880
See, there’s no way of knowing for sure,

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:51.480
but it seems like Valve had to have known that people were using CS:GO skins as gambling chips.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:54.400
It seems there's pretty much no way for them not have known.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:58.480
Plus, some people at the time argued that the growth of CS:GO gambling was

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:00.940
in turn, good for the growth of Valve.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:02.600
Though Valve has denied that.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:08.140
(Stream)

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:10.980
For one, it meant more people were playing CS:GO.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:12.480
Which is good for Valve.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:17.280
but more importantly, Valve takes a 15 percent cut on every sale in the Steam marketplace.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:20.360
So every skin bought is money in Valve's pocket too.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:24.040
And when so many players were rapidly cashing skins in with bots

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:27.240
then cashing them out to turn their liquid asset to a solid one,

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.700
Valve was theoretically raking in cash.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.140
But even Valve can’t stand up to US gambling laws,

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:37.860
and eventually they sent out cease and desist letters to 23 CS:GO skin gambling sites.

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:39.700
Including CS:GO lotto.

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:43.320
The problem is that allegations about Valve’s involvement with skin gambling

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:44.520
ran deeper than that.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:49.120
According to the letter Valve sent out, the sites were violating the Steam Subscriber Agreement by

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:51.040
using Steam accounts for commercial gain.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:54.440
However, you have to think that Valve knew about this.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.880
It wasn’t a secret, just ignored.

00:09:56.980 --> 00:10:00.740
(Stream)

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:05.440
Back to our intrepid scam artists. TmarTn and ProSyndicate managed to avoid any jail time

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:06.800
and didn't even get fined.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:10.720
The FTC let them off with a slap on the wrist. Despite having been found to have paid

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:18.520
other YouTubers and Twitch streamers between $2,500 and $55,000 to promote CS:GO Lotto without disclosing

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:19.440
that relationship.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:24.260
All they had to do was admit they were in the wrong. And they have to disclose any relationship like this in the

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:24.960
future.

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:27.980
But how did TmarTn and ProSyndicate get away with it?

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.080
Well, there was this weird loophole.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:34.320
Speaking to Polygon, TmarTn and ProSyndicate’s lawyer

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:38.320
Coleman Watson explained that CS:GO Lotto wasn’t technically gambling.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:41.120
See, if you looked closely at the terms of use,

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:45.360
CS:GO Lotto said that by using the site, you are acknowledging that you could at any point

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:48.600
ask for an alternative method of entry to any game on the site.

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:51.200
That's right. You didn't have to pay.

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:57.180
If you just read the fine print, you would know that CS:GO Lotto would let you play for free as many times as you

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:57.740
wanted.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:00.680
(Stream)

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:04.440
It’s not like CS:GO’s gambling underground would have lasted that much longer

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:05.800
without all this publcity.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:11.180
TmarTn and ProSyndicate didn't single handedly tank it. This was a massive unregulated, gambling industry

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:12.720
that people were going to notice eventually.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.680
But their dumb scam is why people noticed it when they did.

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:19.100
But while they were driving off into the sunset,

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:22.140
the CS:GO gambling industry was blowing up behind them.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:26.840
They may have burned down the casino, but they still got out of it with all their ill-gotten gains.

